# Fake but well-formed Outscale credentials for the emulator.
#
# The emulator never validates them: authentication is AWS Signature v4 and the
# signature is accepted without being checked, exactly like the other local cloud
# emulators, because the point is to run without an account. The client still
# needs them to be present and shaped right before it will sign anything.
#
# These values are deliberately public placeholders, they open nothing.
#
# Usage: set -a; . tools/conformance/outscale/fake-credentials.env; set +a
OSC_ACCESS_KEY=AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA
OSC_SECRET_KEY=BBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBB
OSC_REGION=eu-west-2
OSC_PROTOCOL=http
# OSC_ENDPOINT_API is deliberately NOT set here.
#
# The Terraform provider and the archived oapi-cli both let the environment win
# over a --config file, so pinning it to 4599 in this file made the [endpoint]
# argument of every Outscale script inert: a run against another port measured
# whatever answered on 4599 — another server, or nothing. An audit reproduced
# it, and the failure looked like a broken emulator (three retries, then a
# timeout) rather than like a misdirected client.
#
# octl inverts that precedence — given --config or --profile it loads the file
# first and merges the environment into what the file left empty (its
# cmd/sdk.go) — which makes a pinned value here harmless for that one client and
# no less wrong for the others. The rule is the same either way, and it is the
# reason this file names credentials and nothing else.
#
# Each script exports it from the endpoint it was given, and calls
# guard_no_real_profile so an unset value refuses to run rather than letting the
# client find the operator's real profile. Note the SHAPE differs by client:
# octl and the Terraform provider >= 1.7 want the /api/v1 path inside the value;
# oapi-cli and the 1.1.x provider want the bare host and append it themselves.
# `feint env outscale --client <family>` prints the right one.
